WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.188 --> 00:00:01.980
SPEAKER 1: Moving from
on-prem to the cloud

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.630
can bring a ton
of nifty features

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.560
for your company
and applications.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.560
But one of the biggest
challenges, and certainly

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.050
the scariest, is how this
movement can potentially

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.960
expose your systems to
new vulnerabilities.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.060
And without taking
the right precautions,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.490
you can run into the risk
of exposing your system

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.540
in very problematic ways.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.210
So in this episode,
I'll be covering

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.760
how to control your traffic
in and out of your instances

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.880
on GCP by creating firewall
rules once you establish

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.920
hybrid connectivity
to Google Cloud.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.877
Stay tuned.

00:00:29.877 --> 00:00:33.146
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.330
Firewall rules are extremely
important for a number

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.570
of reasons.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.560
They allow you to isolate your
internal network and instances

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.930
from unwanted access.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.450
They allow you to monitor
inbound and outbound activity

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.910
coming from your network
for suspicious activity,

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.460
blocking items that are
considered dangerous based

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.500
on a set of security rules.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.450
They establish the
first line of defense

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.790
against attacks,
viruses, and malware,

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.170
and helps create
a secure network.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.830
So let's take a look at
firewalls more closely.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.890
In traditional on-prem
systems, multiple servers

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.930
on a single internal
network are supported

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.150
through the use of a
cluster of firewalls

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.130
coupled with a load balancer.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.890
A large drawback of this
traditional architecture

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.720
is that it doesn't scale well.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.510
In an on-prem environment,
a firewall is generally

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.310
a dedicated piece
of hardware that

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:25.010
has an upper limit
in terms of capacity,

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.470
and this makes a
firewall a choke point.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.270
To support dynamic
scaling, you'll

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.400
need to habitually run
down to the server room

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.380
and replace the
hardware with ones

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.720
that can handle increased load.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.060
Of course, this creates
its own challenge.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.740
When the traffic
goes back to normal,

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.990
you've now got a big piece
of expensive hardware going

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:43.280
unused.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.500
This is where Google Cloud
platform's distributed

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.810
firewalls can make a difference.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.940
Google's global network
has a federation

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.770
of firewalls that
can operate and scale

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.360
as your systems need them.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.280
So you only end up
paying for what you use

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.300
rather than making commitments
for long-term expectations.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.120
This gives you the same power of
your on-site perimeter network,

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.760
which blocks all incoming
traffic by default,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.670
but allows you to scale
without lifting a finger.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.680
Now here's the important part.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.070
Each VPC network functions
as a distributed firewall.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.350
If you want info on
setting up the VPC,

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.380
check out the first episode.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.060
A distributed firewall
means that, by default, it

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.080
will handle filtering traffic.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.040
But you need to adjust it
to handle your access needs,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.920
like applying firewall
rules to tagged instances.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.070
In this example, when a request
comes in from a Compute Engine

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.470
System labeled with
the red tag, it

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.210
hits the applicable
firewall rule

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:38.270
before being allowed to
communicate with the blue tag.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.730
While firewall rules are
defined at the network level,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.840
connections are allowed or
denied on a per-instance basis.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.820
You can think of the
GCP firewall rules

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.400
as existing not only
between your instances

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.130
and other networks, but
between individual instances

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.870
within the same network.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.070
In Google Cloud, a firewall
rule is made up of four things--

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.290
an action either to
allow or deny traffic.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.170
That is, a rule can either
allow incoming, ingress,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.460
or outgoing, egress, traffic,
but not both simultaneously;

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.500
the type of protocol
to which it applies,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.360
such as TCP, UDP, and
ICMP; either a source

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.880
or a destination for
which the rule applies.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.220
This cannot be both as it
depends on the direction

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.750
of the firewall you create.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.700
For example, for
ingress rules you

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.520
would specify the source,
which can be IP ranges, tags,

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.913
or service accounts,
or a combination.

00:03:27.913 --> 00:03:29.330
You wouldn't specify
a destination

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.705
because the rule
is already being

00:03:30.705 --> 00:03:33.260
applied to a specific
VM's inbound traffic.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.260
You can specify the ports
on ingress or egress rules.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.830
For example, allowing ingress
TCP traffic on port 22

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:42.380
to allow for SSH access.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.260
You can also deny a
VM's egress TCP traffic

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.610
on all ports for all
destination VMs in an IP range.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.770
Each of these parameters
means that firewall rules

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.820
can help control traffic to
and from your Google Cloud VMs

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.130
accordingly.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.120
Let's look at what
this looks like.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.710
I have two existing
servers here that

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.800
are trying to use iPerf
to test network speed.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.080
Here are my two SSH
sessions with these VMs

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.610
with iPerf setup.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.310
But note that I have to
use a specific port for it,

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:13.370
and since that's not part of
the standard firewall rules,

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.810
it doesn't work.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.820
The only default
firewall rules created

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.930
are allow egress and deny
ingress traffic, and for Linux

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:25.890
instances, allow SSH/TCP
traffic on port 22.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.350
We're going to create a
new firewall rule that

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.170
allows access for iPerf.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.635
Go to the VPC Network tab
and click Firewall Rules.

00:04:34.635 --> 00:04:37.260
You can see there are a bunch of
default firewall rules created

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.490
for the default network.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.920
We need to create one
for our custom VPC

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.900
that our instances
are sitting in.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.450
So click Add Firewall Rule.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.190
Create a rule iPerf access.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.730
Change the network to VPC 1.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:55.700
Leave it as Ingress
and change it to Allow.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.210
The target tag will
be iPerf access.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:03.270
Source IP range will
be the public internet.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:12.860
The protocol will be TCP and
port will be 5001 to run iPerf.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.650
Once that saves, go back
to the VM Instances page

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.590
and add the iPerf access
tag to Instance 1.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.950
And I'll do the same
now for Instance 2.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.020
Let's run iPerf again.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.330
And boom, it's working.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.340
Firewalls are an
extremely important part

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.223
of your on-prem-to-cloud
transition.

00:06:09.223 --> 00:06:10.640
They're the basis
at which traffic

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.757
can flow to and from
individual cloud instances,

00:06:13.757 --> 00:06:15.590
but also to and from
your on-premise network

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.210
to your cloud network.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.820
If you want to learn about
more complex use cases,

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:21.950
check out the
documentation linked below.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.500
But to get you moving on your
migration to Google Cloud,

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.130
stay tuned for the next
episode on configuring IPs.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.080
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.510
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

